  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nKDDC WSR-88D depicts widespread rain tracking across the western\nCWA early this afternoon. Additional showers and thunderstorms\nare expected to develop later this afternoon, along a CAPE axis\nacross central KS. Severe weather is not expected, but given\ndew points in the upper 60s to low 70s, resulting in increasing\nSBCAPE up around 2,000 J/kg, we expect heavy rainfall to be the\nmain hazard with this activity. The forward progression should\nkeep any hydro issues at bay, but cannot rule out an isolated\ninstance of flooding given antecedent wet grounds. Storms will\nexit the forecast area later this evening. Regarding\nthunderstorm chances tomorrow afternoon, only low POPs (20-30%)\nacross south-central Kansas are in the forecast at this time.\nCAMs struggle to develop much in the way of activity, but will\nnot entirely rule out a storm ahead of a surface trough axis. If\na storm were to develop and sustain, then only marginally severe\nstorms with strong winds and hail would be possible.\n\nDrier and warmer conditions are expected for this weekend across\nthe entire forecast area. High temperatures climb into the 80s\nwith negligible POPs. Into next week, globals deterministic and\nensembles continue to paint another wet period beginning \nMonday, through Tuesday, and into Wednesday. Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning looks to feature the greatest chance at \nsignificant rainfall, as remnant moisture from current powerful \nHurricane Polo track across western Mexico and into New Mexico \nMonday/Tuesday, and eventually into the central Plains Tuesday \nnight. Heavy rainfall seems plausible in this scenario given \nthe strong connection to the tropics. NBM 48-hour probability \nQPF of 1\" or greater is 60-80% across the area, with 2\" \nprobabilities 50-60% for eastern zones. This period will need \nto be monitored for excessive rainfall potential leading to \nareas of flooding.",
